---
description: Fresh Supply code rules — smallest complete change, logic in the database, plain comments. Adapted from ponytail, pstack, thermos and PentAGI.
alwaysApply: true
---
Follow docs/code-rules.md. The short version:

1. Smallest complete change. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config, docs); that is the scope. Reuse ui.js, api.js, config.js and existing CSS. No new dependency, no abstraction or option nobody asked for. Deletion beats addition. A known shortcut gets `shortcut: <limit>, <when to upgrade>`.
2. The database is the boundary. Every write is a SQL function or RLS policy that checks `has_permission(module, action)` and `in_my_scope(city, warehouse)`. UI checks only hide buttons. Business rules are named functions with a one-line "what rule, why" comment. Fixed lists live in config.js and a table / check constraint, never in a screen.
3. Comments say only what the code cannot: why, which constraint, which rule. Never restate the line below. No history. Every file starts with a header.
4. Plain words in docs, UI text and errors. Errors say what to do next.
5. Finish with the review order correct → safe → load → tested → fast → lean (use /fs-review), then `npm test` and the SQL security test (use /fs-security-check).
6. Never: call Supabase from a page, concatenate strings into innerHTML, edit an applied migration, update stock_qty directly, add a framework or build step.
7. A change lands everywhere the thing appears. Lists from a table (sectors, cities, roles, HSN codes, sources) show every active row on every screen; a filter must be a named rule the admin can see and change. Before finishing, grep the thing you changed and walk every hit: screens, exports, fixtures, seed, SQL test, docs. SQL rules and their JS mirrors (`place_order`/`orderPreview`, `compute_price_from`/`computePrice`) change together, with shared test numbers.
8. Never guess a table, column, foreign key or function argument name. Read the migration in supabase/migrations/ (or tests/fixtures/live-schema.json, the live schema). Never make a test pass by adding a wrapper or fixture field the database does not have: tests/schema.test.js fails on any name the live database lacks, and a missing feature needs a migration first.
